 ],
 "literal": "He who drinks, himself, of the fragrant toddy, leaving a little, / on the tongue, many chariots, beautifully reddened, / shining bright, to fade, wilted fresh tumba flowers, / having struck, the blade broken, chipped-edged spears, / with a fragrant chest, of brave war, Akuthai's / deep-water bounded Koodal city like, / thick dark hair and rising breasts, to speak of, / ... / what will happen indeed ... / even if they have prominent thick trunks, the kings' / warfare-skilled elephants to bind, / the roots shook of the trees in our town.",
 "translation": "The chieftain Akuthai, of brave warfare and fragrant chest, drinks fragrant toddy, leaving a little on his tongue. His many chariots are beautifully reddened with blood. The fresh tumba flowers he wore have wilted and faded. His spears have chipped edges and broken blades from striking the enemies. Like his deep-water-bounded city of Koodal is this girl, with thick dark hair and rising breasts. To speak of her... what will happen indeed? Even though the trees in our town have prominent, thick trunks, they tremble to their roots as the kings' battle-trained elephants are tied to them.",
 "modern": "நறுமணமுள்ள கள்ளைக் குடிப்பவன், தன் நாவில் சிறிதளவு மிச்சம் வைத்துச் சுவைக்கிறான். அவனுடைய பல தேர்கள் குருதியால் அழகாகச் சிவந்துள்ளன. அவன் அணிந்திருந்த பசிய தும்பை மாலைகள் வாடிக் குழைந்துள்ளன. பகைவரை எறிந்து இலை போன்ற முனைகள் உடைந்த, வாய் பிளந்த வேல்களைக் கொண்ட மார்பினன், வீரப்போர் புரியும் அகுதை. அவனது ஆழமான நீர் சூழ்ந்த கூடல் நகரைப் போன்றவள் இவள். அடர்ந்த கரிய கூந்தலையும் வளரும் முலைகளையும் உடைய இவளைப் பற்றிப் பேசினால்... என்ன ஆகுமோ? நம் ஊரில் உள்ள மரங்கள் பெரிய பருத்த அடிமரங்களைக் கொண்டிருந்தாலும், வேந்தர்களின் போர் யானைகளைக் கட்டுவதற்காக அவை கட்டப்படும்போது, அவற்றின் வேர்கள் நடுங்குகின்றன.",
 "explanation": "The onlookers or the townspeople are speaking among themselves in this poem of the makaṭpāṟ kāñci theme, which depicts the grief of a father refusing to give his daughter in marriage to kings, leading to war. The beauty of the young girl is compared to the wealthy and well-fortified city of Koodal, ruled by the brave chieftain Akuthai. Because kings have come to demand her hand in marriage and have been refused, they have brought their armies. The impending war is vividly suggested by the image of the kings' battle-trained elephants being tied to the massive trees of the town, causing even those deep-rooted, thick-trunked trees to tremble to their roots. This reflects the terror and destruction that will soon engulf the entire town because of the girl's beauty.",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
 "generated": "2026-11-26"
}